First Author: Chao
Title: Improving prognostic accuracy in lung transplantation using unique features of isolated human lung radiographs
Source number: 8  ￼

Very short summary: This study developed a computer-vision approach to analyze ex vivo lung perfusion (EVLP) radiographs and tested whether radiographic features improve donor-lung prognostication. Five key findings were: (1) CNNs were trained on 1300 longitudinal radiographs from 650 EVLP cases; (2) the best CNN validation performance for donor-lung outcome classification was 66.9% accuracy with AUROC 78.4%; (3) CNN-extracted image features significantly outperformed manually labeled radiographic features in accuracy, while AUROC was similar; (4) the top principal component of the CNN latent space strongly correlated with radiographic infiltration and consolidation; and (5) adding radiographic principal components to the existing InsighTx tabular model significantly improved outcome prediction from 73% to 78% accuracy and from 88% to 94% AUROC. Tables 1-2 and Figures 1-3 were directly used for this extraction.  ￼

